TITLE:: NdefRackLayout
summary:: where the modules of the rack go
categories:: Libraries>NdefRack
related:: Classes/NdefRackGUI, Classes/NdefRack

DESCRIPTION::
The geometry behind the rack window's placing of new modules and its
strong::arrange:: button. It knows nothing of the window or the server: it is
given the modules' boxes - their size and ports - and the cables, and answers
points. The window makes one whenever it places or arranges; you need it only to
lay out modules some other way, or to measure a layout.

The signal flows left to right. In link::#-place:::

list::
## a new module cabled to modules that have a place goes right of what feeds it -
or, when it only feeds, left of what it feeds - level with them, at the nearest
spot that touches no other module. Among spots about as near, the one whose
cables cross fewer cables (link::#*crossing::), run through fewer modules
(link::#*through::) and do not run backwards (link::#*backwards::) wins: each
costs as many pixels of distance.
## new modules cabled only to each other are laid out as a group, the way
link::#-arrange:: lays out a rack, and the group goes in the first free spot
from the top of the area in view, reading left to right.
## a module with no cables is a group of one.
::

Modules that have a place never move.

link::#-arrange:: puts every module in a column by its distance from a source -
a module with nothing patched in in the first, a module fed by it in the next; a
loop is cut where it is found - and orders each column by the mean height of
what its modules are cabled to, sweeping right, then left, twice. That uncrosses
most cables. Modules with no cables at all go in a row under the columns,
wrapping at the columns' width (800 pixels at least).

code::
(
var box = { |params| (size: Point(160, 150), out: Point(144, 135),
	ins: IdentityDictionary.newFrom(params.collect { |p, i| [p, Point(10, 40 + (i * 30))] }.flatten)) };
var boxes = IdentityDictionary[\lfo -> box.([]), \osc -> box.([\freq]), \lpf -> box.([\in])];
var cables = [(from: \lfo, to: \osc, param: \freq), (from: \osc, to: \lpf, param: \in)];
var l = NdefRackLayout([\lfo, \osc, \lpf], boxes, cables);
l.arrange(Point(20, 20)).postln;                       // a row: lfo, osc, lpf
l.place(IdentityDictionary[\lfo -> Point(300, 100)], Rect(0, 0, 1200, 700)).postln;
// -> osc and lpf right of the lfo, level with it
)
::

All the points are whole pixels, on the 10 pixel grid a dragged module snaps to.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
ARGUMENT:: keys
the modules' names, in the order that breaks ties - the window gives
link::Classes/NdefRack#*names::. Names without a box are left out.
ARGUMENT:: boxes
name -> an Event code::(size: aPoint, out: aPoint, ins: (param: aPoint), read: aPoint)::
- the module's size, its output port, an input port per control, and where a
read (an Ndef read inside a source) lands - all in the module's own
coordinates. Without code::read::, a read lands at code::Point(0, 12)::. The
window gives the title's left edge, code::Point(0, 13)::.
ARGUMENT:: cables
Events with code::from::, code::to:: and code::param:: - the shape
link::Classes/NdefRack#*cables:: answers. A cable without a param, an Ndef read
inside a source, goes into the box's code::read:: point. A cable from a module into itself, or to
a name without a box, is left out.
RETURNS:: an NdefRackLayout

METHOD:: gap
the pixels between columns. Default 60, a lane for the flat cables between them.

METHOD:: vgap
the pixels between modules in a column. Default 20.

METHOD:: margin
the room kept around a module that has a place. Default 14.

METHOD:: rowGap
the pixels between the rows of link::#-tidy:: - the lane returning cables run
in. Default 120.

METHOD:: stackGap
the pixels between modules stacked in one column of a row in link::#-tidy::.
Default 60.

METHOD:: backwards
what a cable running from right to left costs a spot in link::#-place::, in
pixels moved from where the module would like to be. Default 400.

METHOD:: crossing
what each cable crossed costs. Default 120.

METHOD:: through
what a cable running through a module costs - one of the new module's through
another, or one already there through the new module. Default 80.

METHOD:: crosses
whether segment a b crosses segment c d. Touching at an end is not crossing.
ARGUMENT:: a
a link::Classes/Point::.
ARGUMENT:: b
a link::Classes/Point::.
ARGUMENT:: c
a link::Classes/Point::.
ARGUMENT:: d
a link::Classes/Point::.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Boolean::

METHOD:: runsThrough
whether segment a b runs through a rectangle.
ARGUMENT:: a
a link::Classes/Point::.
ARGUMENT:: b
a link::Classes/Point::.
ARGUMENT:: rect
a link::Classes/Rect::.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Boolean::


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: place
Places for the modules that have none.
ARGUMENT:: origins
name -> link::Classes/Point::, the top left of every module that has a place.
ARGUMENT:: area
the link::Classes/Rect:: of the rack in view: groups with nothing placed to sit
by go in its first free spot, from its top left.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: name -> link::Classes/Point::,
for the modules not in origins only.

METHOD:: arrange
Places for every module, laid out afresh.
ARGUMENT:: corner
the top left of the layout. Default code::Point(20, 20)::.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: name -> link::Classes/Point::.

METHOD:: tidy
Places for some of the modules, by the layout rules.
Only the cables between the modules of set count.
In each island of them: the main path - the longest chain of audio modules
(each box's code::rate::), ending at a module nothing in the island is fed by -
on one row, a column per stage; an audio module fed by that row and feeding it
(a filter beside a filter) stacked under the stage it runs beside; every other
part - what the cables between the remaining modules join - on a row of its
own under it, the leftmost first, each module right of what feeds it, or left of
what it feeds when nothing feeds it. Islands go one under the other, modules
with no cables in the set in a row under everything.
ARGUMENT:: set
the names to place - any collection that answers code::includes::.
ARGUMENT:: corner
the top left of the layout. Default code::Point(20, 20)::.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: name -> link::Classes/Point::,
for the names in set.

METHOD:: crossings
How many pairs of cables cross, drawn straight from port to port. Cables from
or to the same module are not counted. For comparing layouts.
ARGUMENT:: origins
name -> link::Classes/Point::.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Integer::

METHOD:: keys
the names, as given.

METHOD:: boxes
the boxes, as given.

METHOD:: links
the cables used: between two modules with boxes, never from a module into
itself.
